								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3342.msg37506#msg37506">the merit of Gansta Rap; when is it fruitless &amp; undeserving</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b> on:</b> September 25, 2007, 11:15:23 pm &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">now don&#039;t get me wrong. I can appreciate good quality Gangsta Rap when I hear it, but I think there are some that just shouldn&#039;t be making it regardless of skill<br /><br />Like Rick Ross. I&#039;ve only heard the stuff that gets radio play and makes onto music videos, so please, if he has better stuff let me know<br />but from what I&#039;ve heard of his lyrics, and what I&#039;ve seen of the Videos, it looks to me like his whole image, selling point and lyrical content consists of<br /><br />A) he&#039;s sold a lot of coke<br />B) he&#039;s made a lot of money &amp;<br />C) he likes what he does<br /><br />lyrical skills in mind, should someone like this be making record sales at all?</div>
						</td>
					</tr>
