							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;"><div class="quoteheader"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3342.msg37512#msg37512">Quote from: namski on September 25, 2007, 11:44:09 pm</a></div><div class="quote">from the lyrics of...<br /><br />The Game:<br /><br />A) has had sex with many girls..<br />B) while rolling blunts on there back <br />C) he&#039;s heavily gang affiliated<br />D) raps and claims it openly<br />E) he&#039;s made a lot of money &amp;<br />F) he likes what he does.<br /><br />the shit is just good to listen to sometimes, whodie.<br /><br /><br />...should they be making money off it? well..they almost have no choice but to keep making that music then, do they? supply &amp; demand. <br /><br /><br /><br />Dipset.<br /><br /></div><br />I&#039;m not personally a fan of The Game, but I have heard at least one of his songs that I think had redeeming value<br />the point that I&#039;m trying to make is what about when there is no redeeming&nbsp; value aside from plain lyrical skill in any of an individuals songs, when it&#039;s all about livin it up in the scar face fantasy minus that part at the end when he gets shot up.<br />I mean not all music has a specific message, but with Rick Ross I do hear a message that&#039;s saying &quot;I don&#039;t care how many lives coke fucks up. as long as I&#039;m rich and powerful and I got plenty of tang, I&#039;m happy.&quot;<br /><br />by the way, can someone please explain to me the term &quot;backpacker music&quot; and where the hell it came from</div>
						</td>
