								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3342.msg37524#msg37524"><img src="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/Themes/safgrey105/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://masslinemedia.com/smf/index.php?topic=3342.msg37524#msg37524">Re: the merit of Gansta Rap; when is it fruitless &amp; undeserving</a></b>
									<div class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #11 on:</b> September 26, 2007, 06:58:49 am &#187;</div></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<div style="overflow: auto; width: 100%;">Saba wrote a blog about these issues a little while back. Check it out.<br /><br /><div class="quoteheader">Quote</div><div class="quote">It is far from my intentions to write an overly intellectualized or snooty sounding academic perspective on this interview, so I&#039;ll try to keep this short and simple.<br /><br />There are a lot of folks who come up to us who say &quot;Hey MassLiners, I really like your music because it isn&#039;t about some gangsta gangsta booty shakin bitch ho bling bling crap.&quot;  Sure, part of me appreciates this comment because, in a sense it is true, we don&#039;t like to promote destructive behavior.  I have a mom, I have a sister, I have comrades in struggle that are females; I don&#039;t want, especially as a male, to engage in or promote activity that is oppressive to my brethren/sistren.  But at the same time, when I hear these comments, another part of me feels that whoever says this is missing the point.  I certainly hope folks don&#039;t write off the valuable music and message that other artists are putting out simply because of the image they project or the language they use.  Remember, all music is political.<br /><br />It&#039;s a shame that right now in hip-hop music, the promotion of one idea or image seems to necessitate an implied rejection of all other ideas and images.  Or even more so, how an artist&#039;s acceptance by a certain group of listeners seems to imply that artist&#039;s necessary rejection of other demographics.  <br /><br />I believe that this is the framework folks are operating with when they pay us sideways compliments: &quot;Their music is positive, so they&#039;ll appreciate it if I hate on some thug shit.&quot;<br /><br />That is, so-called &quot;thug shit.&quot;  I challenge anybody to listen through a Blue Scholars or Common Market record and find one line that pretentiously addresses rappers who project a different image.  I adjure them to find a single lyric that puts them down.  We&#039;ve never gone out of our way to insult any artist who works hard to feed their family simply because their style is different from ours.<br /><br />I personally don&#039;t think of our music as &quot;conscious,&quot; but apparently that&#039;s the marketing angle it&#039;s picked up.  That said, so-called &quot;conscious&quot; music should not exclude some of the greats in the rap game now.  MCs that I personally enjoy, that most people seem to think don&#039;t count as conscious, include Lil Wayne, E-40, and TI.  All of these doods, at one point or another, have addressed very legitimate social issues in their music.  Or maybe I should say, &quot;these mahfuccas have talked about some real shit.&quot;<br /><br />Again, this isn&#039;t intended to be another pretentious academic analysis of rap music.  Pieces like that are very condescending and imply that, without an authority to translate a given rapper&#039;s lyrics into &quot;plain english,&quot; it would have no place in civilized discussion.<br /><br />I say that some of the music these rappers put out is filthy because I personally relate to the content.  Yes, some of it is party music, some of it I like because I&#039;ve been conditioned to like it, some if it is plain crap, and yes, some of it is talkin about some real shit.<br /><br />I&#039;m sure I&#039;ve probably said too much.  Besides that, I&#039;m not really the best writer which is why I stick to the beats.  All this yapping is really to introduce this recorded interview of Pimp C.  Midway thru the recording he touches on some points from a perspective that is, what I feel, very similar to where Blue Scholars comes from.  Maybe just in a different style.<br /><br />click here.<br /><br />or go to <a href="http&#58;//www.zshare.net/audio/2833955bb6adc1/" target="_blank">http://www.zshare.net/audio/2833955bb6adc1/</a><br /><br />The majority of rap/hip-hop fans like sound bites like this because they get excited and starry-eyed whenever a rapper acts hella tough; or they love to follow beef like some of these bloggers and journalists out here.  The folks that do this are only those who haven&#039;t lived real life and experienced the serious, sometimes lethal repercussions that talk like this can bring.  If I have criticism for anybody, it&#039;s these types of tourists.  <br /><br />I say &quot;thas wassup&quot; to what Pimp C says because I am also an artist in the public eye that tries to make real music.  Furthermore, I am also an artist that has had to carefully watch the media folks that try to get us to say negative things about our friends and I&#039;m also an artist that has had to cut fake people out of my life and business in order to protect my friends and family.<br /><br />Looks like I failed at keeping it short and simple.  Oh well.  Now all the fools need to get off the booboo!  And word, I wear that same cologne too.<br /><br />Saba</div><br /><a href="http&#58;//masslinemedia.com/news/archive.php?from=40" target="_blank">http://masslinemedia.com/news/archive.php?from=40</a></div>
						</td>
